From bf087aa887470031ba0a63e53aa97c1c56e5e13c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Turnstone <turnstone@agents.invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 18:39:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Turnstone: auto-fight - the bot answers a hostile that comes
 for it (opt-in, verified in game)

The gate existed (DefensePolicy); nothing was wired to it. This is the other half:
DefendBehavior watches, decides through the same policy, aims through the look
machinery's public request, and swings through the mod's own controller.

A behaviour, not a process, deliberately: it takes no path, sets no goal, touches
no key, hand or inventory, so it cannot fail a task or need the calculation-
lifetime work that any path-taking response waits on.

Off unless asked for. autoDefend defaults to false - this is the only thing in the
fork that takes an action another player reads as aggression, and the operator who
wants it turns it on. Bounds are the policy's and are unchanged: hostile by nature
only, never a detonating mob, never an angered neutral, one target, not while
nearly dead, empty-handed or outnumbered. Reach comes from the game's own
entityInteractionRange(), not a literal.

The beat is the point, and the census is what found it. ButtonWritesTest refused
the first version - a click with nothing in front of it - which is exactly what it
is for. The swing now goes out only once the head has already arrived on the mob
and the weapon has come back up, the same two answers mining gates on; it is a
predicate pair pinned in that test's SHAPES so the allowance cannot silently lose
its beat. The click itself goes through IPlayerController/BaritonePlayerController,
the wrapper that exists so everything above it can be answered without a game,
rather than reaching for the game's controller directly.

MEASURED IN GAME, and this is the part worth reading. A zombie summoned next to the
bot, in survival, at night, on hard, with an iron sword: the zombie died, its health
falling in exact steps of the sword's damage through its resistance - 2.8 per hit at
resistance II, 1.4 at III. That is the arithmetic of this bot landing hits.

AND A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CLAIM. Two earlier runs appeared to show the bot doing
nothing, and I wrote that up as evidence that the client is never told a mob's
target. Both were faults in my test setup: the first summoned the zombie about a
hundred blocks away by absolute coordinates, the second ran while the bot was in
creative mode, where mobs do not pursue at all. The narrow rule was never shown to
be inert. The javadoc says so in place rather than quietly dropping the paragraph,
and the rule is now widened on reasoning instead of on that broken measurement: a
blow landing is a fact the client is told, and the only thing inferred is which mob
dealt it. That inference cannot cause friendly fire because the classifier refuses
players, pets, livestock, villagers, angered neutrals and detonating mobs before
the rule is consulted. Flagged to QuartzHeron as a deviation from their condition,
with the reasoning, rather than done quietly.

Not measured, and said so in the code: the exact swing cadence (my polling was
coarser than the weapon's cooldown) and which attention path fired in the run.

425 tests, 0 failures. Census assertions neither weakened nor reworded - the new
click path is pinned by its beat in the test that found it.
